In this lesson we'll look at solving equations when the unknown is hiding inside a logarithm, or tucked up in the exponent. The big idea is really simple: do the opposite operation to set the unknown free. There are two cases to keep in mind. In the first case, the unknown is inside a log. When you spot that, you need to exponentiate both sides, which basically means rebuild the number using its base as a power. That usually gives you a new, friendlier equation to solve. In the second case, the unknown lives in the exponent, like a number raised to the power of x equals something. Here, you take a log of both sides, then use the power law to bring the unknown down so you can solve for it. As a handy shortcut, if the number on the right is already a clean power of your base, you can just match the powers up and skip the logs altogether. So the rule of thumb is this: when the unknown is inside a log, exponentiate, and when it is in the exponent, take logs. Now let's see it in action.